## Migration 016: Add composition column

```sql
ALTER TABLE virtual_models ADD COLUMN composition TEXT DEFAULT NULL;
```

- Default NULL means all existing VMs remain leaf VMs (backward compatible)
- No data migration needed - existing VMs have no composition field

## Go Struct Update

In `internal/models/virtual_model.go`:

```go
// CompositionNode is a recursive tree node for composing virtual models.
// Each node is either a VM reference (Vm set) or an operation (Operation set).
type CompositionNode struct {
    // VM Reference (leaf of the tree)
    Vm string `json:"vm,omitempty"`

    // Operation node (recursive)
    Operation string            `json:"operation,omitempty"` // "union", "intersection", "difference"
    Sources   []CompositionNode `json:"sources,omitempty"`

    // Optional filter/sort applied after this node's operation
    FilterExpr *FilterNode  `json:"filter_expr,omitempty"`
    SortExpr   []SortEntry  `json:"sort_expr,omitempty"`
}

type VirtualModel struct {
    // ... existing fields ...
    Composition *CompositionNode `json:"composition,omitempty"`
}
```

## Validation

In the service layer, validate on create/update:
- If composition is set: recursively validate the tree
  - Each node has either `vm` or `operation`, not both, not neither
  - Operation nodes have 2+ sources
  - `operation` is one of: union, intersection, difference
  - Tree depth does not exceed 10
- If composition is NOT set: filter_expr / sort_expr work as before (leaf mode)
- Mutual exclusivity: if both composition and filter_expr are set at top level -> error
  (filter_expr inside composition nodes is fine - that is the post-operation filter)

## Concrete Use Case: Tiered Subscription Fallback

This section walks through a real-world scenario to validate the composition design.

### Scenario

A user has three subscriptions, each with different cost and quality characteristics:

| Subscription | Provider name | Models | Cost model |
|---|---|---|---|
| Claude Pro | `claude-pro` | Claude Sonnet, Haiku | Subscription (paid monthly) |
| opencode-zen | `opencode-zen` | Various, free tier ending in `-free` | Free (limited quality) |
| opencode-go | `opencode-go` | Various | API credits (paid per use) |

**Behavior desired:**
1. Try Claude Pro first (Sonnet) — best quality, but limited monthly usage
2. When Claude Pro is exhausted or rate-limited, fall back to opencode-zen free models — but only if they meet a quality threshold for the task
3. When free models are exhausted or not good enough, fall back to opencode-go — with quality thresholds tuned per task type

**Different tasks need different quality thresholds:**

| Task | Claude tier | Zen free tier | Go tier |
|---|---|---|---|
| Coding | coding >= 70, hallucination <= 20 | coding >= 75 | coding >= 85, hallucination <= 15 |
| Brainstorming | (any) | (any free) | (any) |
| Planning | intelligence >= 80 | intelligence >= 60 | intelligence >= 75 |

### Provider Setup

```bash
llm-router add-provider --name claude-pro --type anthropic --url ... --key ...
llm-router add-provider --name opencode-zen --url ... --key ...
llm-router add-provider --name opencode-go --url ... --key ...
```

Recommended: add provider metadata for stable filtering:

```
p.name = "claude-pro"       →  p.subscription = "claude-pro",  p.tier = "paid"
p.name = "opencode-zen"     →  p.subscription = "zen",         p.tier = "free"
p.name = "opencode-go"      →  p.subscription = "go",          p.tier = "paid"
```

### Virtual Model Definitions

#### coding-task

```json
{
  "name": "coding-task",
  "description": "Best coding models across all subscriptions, tiered by cost",
  "composition": {
    "operation": "union",
    "sources": [
      {
        "vm": "all-models",
        "filter_expr": {
          "and": [
            { "key": "p.subscription", "op": "eq", "value": "claude-pro" },
            { "key": "mc.coding", "op": "gte", "value": 70 },
            { "key": "mc.hallucination", "op": "lte", "value": 20 }
          ]
        },
        "sort_expr": [{ "key": "mc.coding", "direction": "desc" }]
      },
      {
        "vm": "all-models",
        "filter_expr": {
          "and": [
            { "key": "p.subscription", "op": "eq", "value": "zen" },
            { "key": "m.name", "op": "contains", "value": "-free" },
            { "key": "mc.coding", "op": "gte", "value": 75 }
          ]
        },
        "sort_expr": [{ "key": "mc.coding", "direction": "desc" }]
      },
      {
        "vm": "all-models",
        "filter_expr": {
          "and": [
            { "key": "p.subscription", "op": "eq", "value": "go" },
            { "key": "mc.coding", "op": "gte", "value": 85 },
            { "key": "mc.hallucination", "op": "lte", "value": 15 }
          ]
        },
        "sort_expr": [{ "key": "mc.coding", "direction": "desc" }]
      }
    ]
  }
}
```

#### brainstorming-task

```json
{
  "name": "brainstorming-task",
  "description": "Creative models — quality thresholds are lower, hallucination is tolerable",
  "composition": {
    "operation": "union",
    "sources": [
      {
        "vm": "all-models",
        "filter_expr": { "key": "p.subscription", "op": "eq", "value": "claude-pro" }
      },
      {
        "vm": "all-models",
        "filter_expr": {
          "and": [
            { "key": "p.subscription", "op": "eq", "value": "zen" },
            { "key": "m.name", "op": "contains", "value": "-free" }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "vm": "all-models",
        "filter_expr": { "key": "p.subscription", "op": "eq", "value": "go" }
      }
    ]
  }
}
```

#### planning-task

```json
{
  "name": "planning-task",
  "description": "High intelligence models for planning — moderate quality bar at each tier",
  "composition": {
    "operation": "union",
    "sources": [
      {
        "vm": "all-models",
        "filter_expr": {
          "and": [
            { "key": "p.subscription", "op": "eq", "value": "claude-pro" },
            { "key": "mc.intelligence", "op": "gte", "value": 80 }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "vm": "all-models",
        "filter_expr": {
          "and": [
            { "key": "p.subscription", "op": "eq", "value": "zen" },
            { "key": "m.name", "op": "contains", "value": "-free" },
            { "key": "mc.intelligence", "op": "gte", "value": 60 }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "vm": "all-models",
        "filter_expr": {
          "and": [
            { "key": "p.subscription", "op": "eq", "value": "go" },
            { "key": "mc.intelligence", "op": "gte", "value": 75 }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  }
}
```

### How It Works at Runtime

1. Client sends `model: "coding-task"` to the proxy
2. Proxy resolves `coding-task` — the union returns models ordered: Claude models first, then zen-free models that pass the coding threshold, then go models
3. Proxy tries the first model (e.g., Claude Sonnet). If it succeeds, returns immediately
4. If Claude returns 429 (rate limited), proxy retries up to `max_retries` times with linear backoff (1s, 2s). All Claude models share the same provider, so once retries are exhausted, **all** Claude models are skipped
5. Proxy moves to the next tier: opencode-zen free models filtered by coding >= 75. Tries each in order
6. If zen-free models are exhausted or rate-limited, proxy moves to opencode-go models with coding >= 85
7. If all models fail, returns 502 with a list of failures

### Observations

- **The `all-models` VM** is a helper: a leaf VM with no filter that matches everything. It exists so the composition tree doesn't need to re-specify the full model enumeration. Each source node applies its own filter to narrow down.
- **Task-specific behavior** is achieved by creating separate composite VMs with different thresholds, not by complicating the composition logic
- **The proxy's per-provider retry tracking** naturally handles the tier fallback — once a provider is rate-limited, all its models are skipped
- **Adding a new subscription tier** means adding a new source node to each composite VM — not rewriting the logic
